WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.178 --> 00:00:05.970
ALEX LESTER: Hello.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.780
Thanks so much for coming, and
welcome to our presentation.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.430
Today, we're going
to talk about how

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:13.160
to achieve data-driven
application growth using

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.150
Firebase.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.570
My name's Alex, and I'm
an engineering manager

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.393
on Firebase predictions.

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:19.060
YANLI LI: Hello.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.830
I'm Yanli, a software engineer
on Firebase predictions.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.330
ALEX LESTER: So
let's talk about how

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.240
Firebase can help you, as
an application developer,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.878
grow a successful business.

00:00:29.878 --> 00:00:31.420
We all know that
building a great app

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.780
is something important.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.210
But it's only half the battle.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.450
You want to grow your user
base, retain your users,

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:41.080
monetize them, so you
can grow your business.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.870
So today, we're going to
talk about a few key points

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:45.440
in your journey.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.850
Firstly, how can we objectively
measure your application

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:52.180
success, both in terms of
industry standard metrics

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.940
and events that are unique
to your application?

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.123
Secondly, we'll
be looking at ways

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:58.540
to make changes
to your app's look

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.240
and feel, as well as
behavior, without having

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.372
to do a release to the
various app stores.

00:01:04.372 --> 00:01:07.630
Thirdly, we'll look at ways
to predict user behavior

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.280
using machine learning.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.820
And lastly, we'll be able to
test those changes to ensure

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.840
that there's been
significant impact

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.540
and that the changes
we've made are not

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.030
having any unintended effects.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.390
So let's start with a simple
example of an application

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.100
that you could
build, and then we'll

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.950
adapt it to some more
sophisticated strategies.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:31.270
So let's consider your new
app, Sparky's Adventure.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:34.060
In this app, you've decided
to offer in-app purchases

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.870
to the user while you
continue to develop features

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.240
to make the app more
interesting and appealing

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.100
to a wider user base.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.810
As your app develops
and our team

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.610
becomes more
sophisticated, we try

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.670
to run some tests to help
improve this process,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:53.240
or to ensure the changes that
your team makes don't undo

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.390
any of your previous hard work.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.990
But these tests
can involve a lot

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.390
of one-off engineering
time, which ends up

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.000
being difficult to scale.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.410
But this is where
Firebase can help.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.880
Powerful Firebase back-ends
like cloud functions,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.120
cloud Firestore,
and cloud storage

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.060
allow you to quickly build
and scale your application.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.040
Firebase also has
great tools to make

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.635
sure your app stays
performing and crash-free,

00:02:17.635 --> 00:02:20.830
with products like Crashlytics,
Test Lab, and Performance

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:22.620
Monitoring.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.670
But Firebase can also provide
you with some out-of-the-box

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.730
solutions that can help
you grow your user base,

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.200
retain your users,
and monetize them,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.510
so you can maximize
your business success.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.230
So let's say one day Jane,
your product manager,

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.050
wants to test the hypothesis
that showing the most

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:43.220
expensive items first will lead
to more users spending more

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.760
money per average user session.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.260
Well, that's an
interesting idea.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.340
But it brings with it
a lot of questions.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.060
What other implications
could this change have?

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.050
How would we measure success?

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.100
Let's start by using Firebase
to get some key indicators

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.050
for this app.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.390
First off, we need
to add some analytics

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.772
to our app to measure
the success of our app.

00:03:05.772 --> 00:03:07.730
Now, the nice thing here
is that you've already

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.290
integrated with Firebase.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.070
Analytics is already
up and running.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.850
And if haven't, just by creating
a project in the Firebase

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:18.380
console and adding the base
SDK to your iOS or Android app,

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.120
you can get Analytics
up and running

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.493
without any custom code.

00:03:22.493 --> 00:03:24.160
You get a whole lot
of automatic reports

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.260
right there in the
Firebase dashboard on data

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.610
that's important to us--

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.490
things like in-app
purchases, user engagement,

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.670
retention cohorts, daily
and monthly active users,

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.950
and more, all of
which lets us get

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.890
a good sense of the overall
health of that app right out

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.550
of the box.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:42.940
We will also go ahead and
record any events that

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:44.920
are important to
your application,

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.770
whether that's people
finishing a round in your game,

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:51.040
visiting the in-app store,
starting or completing

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.700
your in-app tutorial,
inviting a friend, and so on.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.920
This is all data that
we can use later,

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:59.740
either in the Firebase
dashboard or through BigQuery

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:02.290
to answer questions that we
might have about our app,

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:04.330
like, what percentage
of my users

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.040
are making it
through the tutorial?

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.510
Is level five too hard?

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:09.080
Is level five too easy?

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.110
Adding these events
needs generally one line

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:14.040
of code per event.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.920
Back to our original idea.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.589
Now that we know that we can
measure the success metrics

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:20.430
we care about.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.930
But how can we
implement this change

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.920
to part of the audience
in a controlled way

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:28.690
and roll back quickly
in case it doesn't work?

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:31.630
How can we make these changes
without an app release?

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.580
You can do it using
Firebase Remote

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:37.210
Config as the primary driver
of changes to your app.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.700
Now, if you've never used
Remote Config before,

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.380
you can think of it
as like a key value

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.720
store that lives in the cloud.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.190
And you can use these values
to change the look and feel

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.050
or behavior of your
app without needing

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:51.350
to publish an update to
the various app stores.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.260
The way Remote Config works is
you set up a bunch of values

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.970
locally in a user's device.

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.040
Then as time goes
on, when you want

00:04:58.040 --> 00:04:59.690
to change one of
these values, you

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:03.650
can do that using Remote Config,
either in the Firebase console

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:07.020
or using our REST API.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.330
Then, the next time the
user starts up the app,

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:11.310
we will download these
values and apply them

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.440
on top of the default values.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.210
When we ask for a
value, you'll likely

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.150
get the default value that
we supplied initially,

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.010
or one of the new values we've
downloaded from the cloud.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:22.670
And this is nice,
because it means

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.590
that we can have hundreds of
values wired up and ready to go

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:26.770
through Remote Config.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:28.770
But because you're only
downloading the deltas--

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:30.420
the values that have changed--

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.850
you don't have to worry about
excessive downloads or data

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:36.440
usage every time a
user starts the app.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.220
So for instance, if you've gone
ahead and parameterized a sort

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.830
order of the items in the
store as a variable in-app,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:47.710
this is something we can update
easily through Remote Config.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:50.090
You can push our
update to the world,

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.613
potentially only for a small
fraction of our audience.

00:05:52.613 --> 00:05:54.280
And if there's a
problem, you can simply

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:58.450
come back to Remote Config and
undo the change that we made.

00:05:58.450 --> 00:05:59.538
OK.

00:05:59.538 --> 00:06:01.080
So you have the
change ready to ship.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.040
But wait.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.830
Jane has had a new idea.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.830
Jane is wondering if
you can change the sort

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:09.890
order to show the most expensive
items first, but only do it

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.290
for the people who have spent
money in our application

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:13.690
before.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:16.460
Her hypothesis is that
this will maximize revenue

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.970
among your spenders, while
still enticing our non-spenders

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.570
to spend money by showing
them more affordable options.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:26.000
This is something we can do
pretty easily with the systems

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.550
that we already have in place.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.110
When you implement
analytics, you

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:32.910
have the ability to
create audiences.

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:37.430
These are users who meet some
set of criteria in your app,

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:41.370
based on triggering events or
being assigned to various user

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:42.790
properties.

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:46.100
And one audience that analytics
creates automatically for you

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:46.970
is purchasers.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:53.862
This consists of anybody who
has ever spent in your app.

00:06:53.862 --> 00:06:56.320
One of the nice features about
Remote Config that I haven't

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.000
mentioned yet is
that it contains

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.280
logic that allows you
to send different values

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.350
to different users,
depending on their situation.

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.620
So for instance, you can
send different values

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.620
to your Canadian users,
either for a nice localized

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:09.800
experience--

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:13.750
Sparky's Canadian Adventure--
or test a new feature in Canada

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.180
before rolling it out to
the rest of the world.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.340
But you can also deliver
a different experiences

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.640
based on analytics data,
like whether a user is

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.650
in a certain audience.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:26.630
So you can tell
Remote Config, hey,

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:30.200
let's create a special condition
based on whether or not people

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:33.180
are in the purchasers audience.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:34.790
And then you can
tell Remote Config

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:39.110
to change your sort order
only for people in this group.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:42.430
And you get to implement Jane's
new monetization strategy, all

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:45.100
without touching any of
the code in your app.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:48.690
While this makes Jane happy,
it also gets her thinking.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:52.970
What if our past purchasers
tend not to spend money again?

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:56.390
Thus we should focus on racking
expensive items more highly

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:59.690
only for future
purchasers, meaning

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.850
those that have not
purchased but will likely

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:04.160
purchase in the future.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:07.280
Can we really
predict the future?

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.600
Yes, you can, with a little
help from machine learning.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:14.330
And we're going to do this by
using Firebase Predictions.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:18.720
Let me hand it over to
Yanli, who can teach us how.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.060
YANLI LI: Thanks, Alex.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:20.780
Yes.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.610
With Firebase
Predictions you can

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:25.070
apply the power of machine
learning to segment

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:29.650
you are users based on their
predicted future behaviors.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:32.840
The general idea is that as
your users are interacting

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.080
with your app, the day getting
sent to Google Analytics

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:37.789
for Firebase.

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:40.200
Once enabled,
Firebase Predictions

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.409
will look at our data and
learn from the patterns

00:08:43.409 --> 00:08:48.240
that your users exhibit before
they perform certain actions.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:51.426
So let's dig a little
deeper into what we do.

00:08:51.426 --> 00:08:54.990
It's actually-- this is a
supervised learning problem.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:57.680
The first thing is to
come up with examples

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.230
to teach a model
what it is exactly

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.310
that you want it to learn.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:05.580
The motto is updated
every day, so every day we

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:09.380
take the last 45 days
of user activity data,

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:12.810
reserve the last 7 days
as the labeling window,

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:16.620
and the past 38 days
as the feature window.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:18.870
In the labeling
window, we figure out

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:22.470
what actually happened and
give labels to the users,

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:26.482
such as churner, spender,
reviewer, et cetera.

00:09:26.482 --> 00:09:28.650
And in the future we
know we [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:33.860
features that can represent
the user's behavior patterns.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.150
The data is separated into
training set and evaluation

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:38.110
set.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:41.080
We feed the training set
into a neural network powered

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:43.590
by TensorFlow for
training the model.

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:46.440
Then, we apply the model
to the evaluation set,

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.650
to generate statistics about
quality of the predictions,

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:55.360
such as precision, recall,
accuracy, et cetera.

00:09:55.360 --> 00:10:00.180
And then we expose some of
these metrics on the UI.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.450
Once the model
quality is verified,

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:05.430
we are ready to apply the
model on our latest user data

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:09.410
to generate prediction
scores [INAUDIBLE]..

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:12.860
There, you can target them
in the Firebase products

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:15.100
that you typically
would use, like

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:17.810
for instance, Remote Config.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:20.340
So how do you use
Firebase Predictions?

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:22.120
Do you need to go
back to the university

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:25.290
and spend several semesters
taking on neural networks

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:27.660
for learning class?

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:28.420
No.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:31.450
You just need to click this
button here on the Predictions

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:33.990
console, and that's really it.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:37.260
At this point, Predictions
will start a model training

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:41.340
and evaluation process for the
two [INAUDIBLE] predictions,

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.290
spend and churn.

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:46.036
So by default, we
will predict who

00:10:46.036 --> 00:10:49.230
will spend money in
your app, or who is

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:51.570
likely to stop using your app.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.980
But really, Firebase
Predictions can

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:57.920
be set up to predict around
any that you have in your app,

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:01.020
like who's going to
create accounts or share

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:04.770
the app with their friends.

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:08.290
Creating customized
predictions is very simple.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:10.950
You just need to click the
Create a Prediction box

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:14.200
in the Predictions
console, and then

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:18.390
provide a name so you can
easily find it later and choose

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:23.550
the specific analytics that
you want to predict for.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:25.380
Then, in a couple
of days, you'll

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:28.700
start to get the predictions
that you can work with.

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:29.400
Let's see.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:31.820
Now the UI shows
your submit a review

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:34.040
prediction is ready to use.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:37.770
By clicking this Explore
and use prediction button,

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.740
you'll be able to
select a threshold

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:44.440
to control the audience size
and prediction probability.

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:46.870
On this Select
user segment page,

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:50.160
you can expand a graph
of [INAUDIBLE] users.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.900
They are sorted from left
to right in how likely they

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:57.550
are to submit reviews.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.720
The probability score
is on the y-axis.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:04.080
We can use these blue
sliders to select a segment.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:08.000
And as we move the sliders, you
will see the bars and the data

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.090
change on the right
to give us more

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:12.200
information about
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:15.010
that we are selecting.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:16.810
After selecting
the segment, you'll

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:19.540
be able to choose where
to use the prediction

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:23.310
with other Firebase tools,
such as Remote Config, In-App

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:27.020
Messaging, and Cloud Messaging.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:31.860
And that's how easy it is
to use Firebase Predictions.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:34.100
Now you can leverage
the power of machine

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:35.850
learning in your app.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.180
But Jane's just
thinking, what if I

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:40.490
want to dive deep into my data?

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:43.310
Or combine with my
in-house data source?

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:47.160
Or export it to a
third party system?

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:50.310
Firebase and BigQuery
have you covered.

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:53.760
Many of Firebase tools already
integrate with BigQuery,

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.590
including Predictions.

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.290
To export your Predictions
data to BigQuery,

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:02.800
just go into your settings
and find BigQuery integration

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:04.576
[INAUDIBLE].

00:13:04.576 --> 00:13:06.890
The BigQuery
integrations page allows

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:10.470
you to easily manage all
of your links in one place,

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:15.270
turn them on and off, and
[INAUDIBLE] data set, easy.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:17.190
After linking your
app, data set will

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:21.780
be created for you are
visible from the BigQuery UI.

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:25.720
From here, you can [INAUDIBLE]
against your raw and sample

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.120
data you can also
export or join your data

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:35.340
sets with external data sources
to perform custom analysis.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:37.245
So going back to Jane's request.

00:13:37.245 --> 00:13:40.165
We are going to change that
condition in Remote Config

00:13:40.165 --> 00:13:42.540
to now include
users who we think

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:46.060
will spend money in the future.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:50.990
Let's change a condition to
be based on spend predictions,

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:53.630
then further adjust
the percentile of users

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:55.560
will be included.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:59.010
For example, only
the top 1% users

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:02.354
who are most likely to
spend in the future.

00:14:02.354 --> 00:14:05.910
Hit the Save button, and then
change the sort_order parameter

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:07.830
value in a way
that you think will

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:12.200
generate the most of that
revenue for these users.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:13.070
Great news.

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:17.055
The new sort order for future
spender is ready to ship.

00:14:17.055 --> 00:14:19.670
But Jane starts thinking again.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:23.692
How can we be sure that this
has the intended outcome?

00:14:23.692 --> 00:14:26.550
Even if we saw an
uptick in spending,

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:29.120
how do we know that it
was due to the changes

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.360
we just made instead
of some random chance

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:35.270
or some other change
in our app or maybe

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:39.170
a promotion we were
running at the time?

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.860
Many apps have a variety
of success criteria.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:44.570
Let's look at ways
that you can measure

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:47.160
the impact of your
actions accurately,

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:50.630
as well considering any
unintended consequences

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.930
of your change.

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:56.220
This is what Firebase
A/B testing is for.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:00.040
A/B Testing helps make sure that
all organizations in your app

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:05.640
are backed up by actual data
and the statistical analysis.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.330
So let's try to prove
Jane's hypothesis by data

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:12.060
by creating our A/B test.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:14.000
Let's go ahead and
Create experiment

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.610
on the A/B Testing console.

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:19.766
Let's choose to
use Remote Config.

00:15:19.766 --> 00:15:22.040
In our case, their
target audience

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.760
is going to be users
who are predicted

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:27.450
to spend money in your app.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:31.830
We can run experiment on,
say, 30% of these users.

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:34.040
Then you can tell
Firebase that you

00:15:34.040 --> 00:15:38.290
want to maximize in-app
purchase revenue as our goal.

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:40.000
Note that Firebase
can also check

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.040
a number of secondary goals.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:44.200
This is very useful
for determining

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:46.670
the overall health of your app.

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:49.150
If you made a change that
boosted your in-app purchase

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:52.780
revenue that was so aggressive
that it lowered your user

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:56.350
retention, that would
be important to know.

00:15:56.350 --> 00:16:00.500
So you secondary goals
can help you check that.

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:04.060
In the Variants section, you can
add a few different parameters

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.520
values here.

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:11.350
You can have up to 70
different variants if you want.

00:16:11.350 --> 00:16:13.410
Then you can check back
in a couple of weeks

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:15.650
and see what happens.

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:18.220
This is what a sample
report might look like.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:21.565
These reports come with a
lot of tables and charts.

00:16:21.565 --> 00:16:24.430
But the most important
takeaway is this line here

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:26.100
on top of the page.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.880
It tells us that
for this experiment,

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:33.030
we have 99.9% confidence
that variant A, which

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:36.190
is ranking expensive items
higher for future spenders,

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:39.560
will increase both estimated
[INAUDIBLE] revenue and user

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:41.700
retention.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:44.430
So this is great we
tested our hypothesis

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:47.130
using experiments and data.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:51.570
Now, let me hand back
over to Alex to wrap up.

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:52.800
ALEX LESTER: Thanks, Yanli.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:54.883
It's worth pointing out
that just about everything

00:16:54.883 --> 00:16:56.070
we talked about today--

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:59.490
whether it's using Remote Config
to change how your app behaves

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:03.060
in different situations or
changing how your app behaves

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:06.869
against users predicted to
hit some conversion event,

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:10.650
running an A/B test to
improve your purchase revenue,

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.500
or perhaps running a
cloud messaging campaign

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:17.099
to tell users about your
next big sale, all of this

00:17:17.099 --> 00:17:19.560
can be done by
anyone on your team

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:21.420
directly through the
Firebase console,

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.770
as long as you grant
them permission.

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:25.960
So once your engineering
team has done the work

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:28.560
to set up each of these
features, your growth

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:31.000
team, your game
designers, or others

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:32.740
that want to help
you succeed can

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:35.620
run all of these experiments
or marketing campaigns

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:38.230
directly through the
Firebase console,

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:40.890
while your engineering
team can go off and work

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:43.100
on the next big thing.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:45.160
And your new app's
monetization strategy

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:48.440
gets to go from a very simple,
provide in-app purchases

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:51.220
and keep pushing more
features kind of a strategy,

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:53.560
to a data-driven,
fully-optimized monetization

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:54.355
strategy.

00:17:54.355 --> 00:17:58.010
It creates audiences to get
metrics like Google Analytics,

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.080
applies different parameter
values to different audiences,

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:02.740
applies different
parameter values

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:06.850
based on predictive behavior,
and maximizes in-app purchase

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:10.205
revenue through A/B Testing.

00:18:10.205 --> 00:18:12.580
So I hope you've learned more
today about data-driven app

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:13.660
development.

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:18.010
To sum up-- one, we measure
our overall app health

00:18:18.010 --> 00:18:19.690
and set custom measures.

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:22.490
We can use Google
Analytics for Firebase.

00:18:22.490 --> 00:18:25.000
Two, to make changes on
device without pushing

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:27.000
a change through
the app store, we

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.674
can use Firebase Remote Config.

00:18:29.674 --> 00:18:32.800
Third, to predict
user behavior, we

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:35.380
can leverage
Firebase Predictions.

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:38.230
Four, to prove our
success, we can

00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:41.463
leverage Firebase A/B testing.

00:18:41.463 --> 00:18:43.630
If you're interested in
learning more about anything

00:18:43.630 --> 00:18:47.860
we've talked about today or
any other user application

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:49.840
growth-related
questions, we'd love

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:51.520
to hear from you on the Dory.

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:53.250
Thank you.

